  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Chances of showers and thunderstorms through \nSaturday. A few thunderstorms may produce locally heavy rain and \nstrong gusty winds.\n\nAn ill-defined wavy frontal boundary remains stalled just to our \nwest and northwest, with very little movement expected today. The \nnext feature of interest is a convectively augmented shortwave over \nIndiana-Ohio, which will track northeast towards the region along \nthis boundary this morning. CAMs continue to suggest that with this \nfeature showers and maybe even some embedded thunderstorms will \narrive across far western New York between 10Z-13Z. There remains a \nfair amount of uncertainty surrounding the evolution of this feature \nas it moves east and northeast across the region this afternoon. \nThere definitely will be plenty of moisture, with PW values found \nbetween 1.7\" to 1.9\" range. Model MLCAPE shows +1200 J/kg inland \nfrom the lakes, with much higher values east of the Finger Lakes \nregion. This may be due to potential cloud cover across our CWA \nwhich may limit storm development this afternoon and eve. That \nsaid...if we do see a fair amount of sunshine we may see additional \nstorms form along lake breeze convergent boundaries. Any of the \nstronger cells that form will be capable of produce heavy rainfall \nwithin this soupy environment and possibly strong gusty winds. Given \nthe environment WPC continues to highlight our region in a marginal \nrisk for excessive rainfall. \n  \nTonight...some showers may linger across the region, especially east \nof Lake Ontario. Otherwise...another muggy night is on tap with lows \nfound in the 60s to around 70F. \n  \nAnother wave is advertised to move through the area Saturday morning \nbringing the next round of showers and thunderstorms. After that...a \ncold front approaches from the northwest Saturday eve with \nadditional chances for showers and thunderstorms, then showers will \nslowly wind down as we head into Sunday.   \n\nKey Message 2...Drier and less humid airmass briefly settles over \nour region Sunday.\n\nSurface high pressure quickly races east Sunday, which will provide \ndry weather and pleasant conditions (lower humidity levels) across the \nregion  \n\nKey Message 3...Unsettled weather returns for the Lower Great Lakes \nMonday through Tuesday.\n\n\nA zonal flow early next week will begin to shift southward, with a \nmore noticeable dip late in the week as a deepening upper level low \ndrops southward from Canada. \n\nA moisture rich airmass will remain through the beginning of next \nweek, one that will support not only afternoon convection, but the \npossibility of overnight convection (Monday night into Tuesday), \nthough exact timing of subtle shortwave troughs is with still great \nuncertainty at this point in time. As the cooler and drier upper \nlevel low settles over the eastern Great Lakes region chances for \nshowers and thunderstorms will begin to diminish once the drier \nairmass becomes established.",
